rying to put  a lot of pressure on this central e4  square that we talked about earlier then  we can castle on the King side still and  we'll have this semi open F file if we  can get off this pawn on f5 but as you  can see this is very aggressive you're  really trying to put a lot of pressure  on the center especially this e4 square  so you definitely do have a lot of  options in the Rubinstein system one of  the variations is getting a lot of  traction in chess is Queen to c2 a lot  of white players when they face the  nimzo-indian defense which is very  common they really don't want to have  these double pawns on the C file so a  lot of times instead of opting for the  Rubinstein with pawn to e3  they'll just instead play Queen to c2  basically saying if you want to trade  off your Bishop pair that's fine but I'm  just going to recapture with my Queen  here on c3  now there are a few main lines that you  will see the most common is castling on  the King side and then pawn to a3 really  trying to test black and see what  they're going to do if he's going to  capture and give up his bishop here do  something else from here black is going  to capture he's going to be very passive  if he tries to bring his bishop back to  a5 and then  white can continue to push his pawns  down the board and eventually force  black to lose material as he's going to  lose his bishop here now from here why  it's obviously going to take as we  talked about before with this Queen on  c3 then we're going to bring our pawn to  b6 getting right into P and headed with  our Bishop to b7 and start to attack  this Square on e4 here now from here  black can bring his Bishop down to g5  pinning our knight down to our queen as  you can see here already White's pieces  are a little bit more active as far as  ours have not gone past the six rank but  at the same time this complete side his  King side is completely underdeveloped  and most of ours we already have King  side safety we're already Castle down  the king side at the same time we can  develop our LightSquared Bishop pretty  easily we can get our knight developed  pretty easily with Knight to c6 so black  does have a lot of options here and this  is kind of a common theme that you will  see is black really tries to play a  little passive early on tries to build  up a nice defense and then counters  later on the next variation that you  will see and again this we saw this in  the Rubinstein but it's pawn to c5 and  this is again my favorite if I do face  this Queen to c2 because it really right  away puts a lot of pressure on this b4  pawn there's not a lot white can do to  hold on to both his epon and his deep on  here now if he does capture we do have a  few options like before we can bring our  knight to a6 and capture with our knight  we can ride away capture with our bishop  or if we want to we can capture this  night here on c3 and then bring our  knight to a6 and capture on c5 so we  definitely do have a lot of options I  prefer just to bring our Bishop down to  c5 and from here we can develop our  Knight to c6 if we want to we can castle  on the King side and I feel like this  has a very good game for black in store  the laceration that you will see is the  kasparov and that's Knight to f3 now  it's not played very often so I'm not  going to spend too much time on it but  from here just keep in mind that if you  do see it you really want to start  counter attacking the center right away  and you can do that by pawn to d5 again  putting a lot of pressure on this  efore square at the same time really  challenging white and the center here we  can eventually as we talked about before  bring your pawn to c5 then we can start  to develop other minor pieces castle on  the King side but right away we're  basically saying you know we're going to  try to attack this Center control that  White's already build up so if you do  see this knight come to f3 don't panic  but you won't see it as much as the  Rub